WEBVTT

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:07.700
<i>Though the spring sunshine may
herald the coming of summer,</i>

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:13.520
<i>some can only let out a cold, wintry breath,
trapped in the dark as they are.</i>

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.240
<i>How can they be offered salvation?</i>

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.990
<i>How can they be afforded the
warm touch of a gentle melody?</i>

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.070
<i>Tell me how, Haruta.</i>

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.010
<i>I'm Homura Chika,</i>

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:09.160
<i>a cute girl in the first year of high school,
and a wonderful flutist.</i>

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:11.770
Huh?!

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.220
<i>Okay, that part was a lie. I only picked up
the flute once I started high school.</i>

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.280
<i>I'm a beginner with a mere
month and a half of experience.</i>

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.780
This is still too hard for you, huh?

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:20.920
Don't worry!

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.200
Your playing is nice and loud.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.790
Be a good girl and hand out
these flyers, Chika-chan.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:27.780
You're nothing if not loud, so there's that.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.880
What was that for?

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:38.210
Good morning.

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:39.860
Has anyone asked to join?

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.770
Nobody yet.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.760
But we've had a few stop to listen to us.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:45.970
We have a chance, then.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:47.170
Yes.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:49.080
Let's take it one more time from the top.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:50.560
Right.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.030
We are the Concert Band!

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.650
Please consider joining us!

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:54.940
Here you go!

00:02:55.580 --> 00:03:00.570
We are the Concert Band!

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:01.650
Please take one.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.200
We can put on fun
performances together—

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:03.620
Knock it off!

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.310
Narushima Miyoko, Class 1-3.

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.840
You know of her?

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:18.670
Somewhat.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.880
She's an outstanding talent
I want in our band at all costs.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.760
In junior high, she played the oboe
on stage at Fumon Hall.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:29.550
What's more,

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.840
her band only had twenty-three members—
an exceptionally low number.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:36.560
Despite that, their debut performance
earned them silver in the competition.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.560
Huh? Twenty-three is an
exceptionally low number?

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:40.810
What are you talking about?

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:41.980
Of course it is.

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.570
The All-Japan Concert Band Competition
is divided into two categories:

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:47.250
Category A, which allows
a max of fifty-five members,

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:49.800
and Category B, which allows a max of thirty.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.700
But Category B only goes up to the regional level.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.060
You can't participate in the All-Japans
at Fumon Hall that way.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.750
Just how many people will we have
to recruit before the competition?

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.350
It's not like we can compete this year regardless.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.840
We'd have a hard time even
in Category B with our roster.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:09.430
Would you like to listen to
Narushima-san's performance?

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:12.470
This is your place?

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.810
Yeah. I live alone in this apartment complex.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.700
What? Really?

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:21.310
Instead of having my own room,
I got an old apartment.

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.440
I split the rent with my parents.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:24.650
Talk about living in comfort.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.020
You remember my older sisters, right?

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.880
Minami-san, Tsumugi-san, and Fuyuna-san...

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.830
That place is hell.

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:35.290
Come in.

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.580
Thanks for having me!

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.470
Wait, isn't this a bad idea?

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:42.300
Should I really just waltz into the
apartment of a boy who lives alone?

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.670
I have zero interest in you,
for your information.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:45.760
I know.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:50.510
This was at last year's junior high All-Japans.

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:52.600
Wow.

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.800
But don't you think she has
a problematic personality?

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.330
What are you saying?

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.280
If we're going to cover for your mistakes,

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.130
we need as many good performers as we can get.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.510
Figures.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.780
I want us to take part next year.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:14.760
I want to help Kusakabe-sensei take the stage
at Fumon Hall as a conductor.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:15.580
Yeah...

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:18.850
We need her skills to achieve that.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.250
I'm done with concert bands, sorry.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.630
And that's a crying shame, isn't it?

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.800
I'm sure your oboe must be
sobbing its heart out.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.970
Yeah! It's crying so much it's got
its own, even smaller violin!

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.680
Chika-chan, you're way off.
In so many ways.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.100
Take your comedy act elsewhere.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.200
Please! Help us out here!

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.480
We have every intention of
making it worth your while.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:41.860
Tell us what you want.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:42.840
For you to go away.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:43.660
Other than that.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.150
I'm thirsty.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:50.870
Where are we supposed to find
domestic pineapple juice?!

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:52.450
Here it is!

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:53.990
Chika-chan, money!

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.580
Just you wait, Princess Kaguya!

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:00.590
Here you go, Narushima-san.

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:09.990
Hey, you said you wanted this!

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:11.090
Sorry.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:15.220
It's our bad for disturbing
your peaceful school life.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:19.890
Going to the market was something
we decided on our own accord,

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:23.900
and while Chika-chan has absorbed the cost
of the juice, she doesn't hold it against you.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:25.190
How much was it?

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:26.630
How much?

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:28.060
Quite a lot.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.190
After all, it was expensive
domestic pineapple juice.

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:34.960
But you nearly bought
domestic kiwi fruit juice.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.740
I love kiwi fruit.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:39.810
Did you know the kiwi fruit is in the
genus Actinidia, just like cat powder?

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.260
Really? Maybe my cat will like it, then.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.080
So how much was it?

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:47.500
I don't care about the money.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:49.280
Sorry. I apologise.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.560
If we want you to join the concert band,
we should just come out and say it.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:57.450
You're annoying me.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:03.730
The way she's acting,
we won't get along even if she does join.

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:06.400
Something's not right.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:09.520
Don't you sense anything?

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:10.640
Not really.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:11.830
Thought as much.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.230
"He who would the daughter win,
must with the mother first begin."

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.660
What? No way!

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:22.580
But Koromin said so!

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:24.500
That's just out of the question, right?

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:25.290
Right!

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:28.380
You're Nishikawa Mayu-san, right?

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:30.090
Y-Yes.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:31.910
Do you have a moment?

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:34.490
Sorry to make you come here.

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:35.930
Don't be!

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:37.980
You were friends with Narushima-san, right?

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:42.680
I noticed you two walking home together in April.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:45.620
You've had your eye on her for that long?

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:50.110
Narushima-san moved here recently,
so she doesn't have any friends from junior high.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:54.690
Going by roll numbers,
you would've ended up sitting behind her.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.150
You struck up conversation first, right?

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:59.410
A lot of people become friends that way.

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:01.830
Wow! Just who are you, Kamijou-kun?

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:02.920
A moron.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:07.920
But I bet you couldn't handle her
control-freak nature after a while.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.320
You shouldn't let it bother you.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.220
That's how oboe players are fated to be.

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:19.310
The oboe changes its tune drastically
based on its player's quirks.

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.050
It's a very sensitive instrument.

00:08:21.710 --> 00:08:23.830
So the longer one plays it,

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:25.430
the more of a control freak they become.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:27.360
Is that so?

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.140
Are you serious?

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:29.750
But you know what?

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:33.760
That's proof that she's given the oboe her all.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:37.660
We would love to have her as a bandmate.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:40.960
But Miyo-chan won't...

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:45.320
You know why she quit the oboe, don't you?

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:51.460
I was there when she played
at All-Japans last year.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.380
After all the programs had ended,
a scream rang across the auditorium.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:02.840
And she was nowhere to be seen
during the awards ceremony.

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:05.070
Does that have anything to do
with why she quit?

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.810
Miyo-chan's little brother
passed away that day.

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:27.920
Miyo-chan's little brother had
been in hospital for a long time.

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.750
His condition took a
sudden turn for the worse.

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:32.450
Their parents were at the venue as well,

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:34.720
so there was nobody by his side at the end.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:38.710
So she felt guilty and quit the oboe?

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:39.850
Yeah.

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:41.630
Have you ever gone to hang out?

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:44.640
At Narushima-san's place.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:49.730
Such a lovely aroma.

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:51.010
Could it be beef stew?

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:52.400
Yes?

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:53.070
It's Nishikawa.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.180
Oh, welcome!

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.070
Just one moment!

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.070
Long time no see.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:00.160
Help yourselves.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.910
Sorry for showing up without notice.

00:10:02.330 --> 00:10:03.370
Don't be.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:06.580
It's been such a long time since
any of Miyoko's friends stopped by.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.880
Um, where's Miyo-chan?

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:11.960
I'm sorry. I called for her, but...

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:16.330
Oh, it's our fault for dropping in
without telling her.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:19.570
We have plenty of time,
so we'll wait as long as it takes.

00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:22.290
I even brought along
my own chopsticks, just in case.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.740
And Chika-chan was interested
in some of that beef—

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.170
What was that for?

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:29.810
Miyo-chan...

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:32.780
You can drink that in my room.

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:35.740
The coffee.

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.240
Sorry, Miyo-chan.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:40.120
This is a bother, isn't it?

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:41.660
That should go without saying.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:43.860
Drink your coffee and go home.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:45.730
Don't you know?

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:49.790
Haruta once spent five hours
on one cup of coffee at a café

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:52.630
just so he could finish reading
eighty-three volumes of Patalliro!

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:53.630
You have five minutes.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:56.420
She says we have five minutes, Haruta.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:59.510
It's like a little museum of sorts.

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:00.640
I'm lucky I got to see this.

00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:02.070
What do you mean?

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.910
These are all puzzles.

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:06.350
All the classics are in here.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:10.260
And the pictures on the wall
are all trompe l'oeils.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:12.990
I doubt you collected all of this.

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:14.210
Why is that?

00:11:14.210 --> 00:11:17.560
These books are the Bibles for puzzle buffs.

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:21.410
Their author, Dudeney, was a genius
whose talents manifested at the age of nine.

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:26.000
Looking at this "exhibit",
I find it hard to believe you touch them often.

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:29.000
I bet they all belonged
to your little brother.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:30.890
So what?

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.270
It's fantastic.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:38.360
He must've admired Dudeney,
who was already known as a genius at his age.

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:42.760
Look, he signed all the items he made himself.

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:47.230
His devotion to puzzles was truly praiseworthy.

00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:50.440
As serious as his illness may have been.

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:52.360
What is with you?

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:57.570
Everything you have here is the mark
your brother left on the world.

00:11:57.570 --> 00:11:58.900
They're truly precious,
and not for show.

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:02.330
Did you actually solve all of them?

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:05.120
I'm not surprised.

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:07.150
You couldn't have.
Not in your current state.

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:08.500
That should go without saying.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:10.130
Haruta!

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:14.790
Because you're carrying a burden that you
can never lighten, and you're doing it alone.

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:17.880
And what could you possibly do?

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:21.040
We can help shoulder it together with you.

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:23.840
We can try to solve any puzzle that you can't.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:28.040
At the very least, you won't be the only one
suffering due to the puzzles in this room.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:31.180
Get out.

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:33.480
Get out!

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:35.360
Sorry.

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:53.580
I've listened to Miyo-chan's
All-Japans performance.

00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:56.020
Were you at the venue as well?

00:12:56.020 --> 00:12:58.690
No, I listened to the recording.

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:01.760
Satoshi-kun had asked
Mr. and Mrs. Narushima.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:04.250
He told them to watch over
Miyo-chan on stage

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:07.470
and record her performance for him,
or else he'd hate them forever.

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:09.290
Oh...

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:10.350
Nobody was in the wrong.

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:13.240
It was just a perfect storm of bad luck.

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.080
So it was nobody's fault.

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:22.250
Miyo-chan?

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:25.800
Try to solve this by yourselves.

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:28.030
A Rubik's cube?

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:30.700
All of its faces are white.

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:37.000
Out of all the puzzles Satoshi left behind,
this is the only one I just can't solve.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:39.530
Is this scrambled?

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:40.880
"Scrambled"?

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:42.470
It's when a puzzle is jumbled up
and ready to solve.

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:43.860
He said it was.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.550
And the solution?

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:47.510
He didn't show me.

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:50.450
If we solve this,

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:54.350
will you seriously consider
joining the concert band?

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.520
You have until after school on Friday.

00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:01.030
Got it.

00:14:01.640 --> 00:14:03.030
You can't do it.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:05.400
That cube is impossible to solve.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:09.940
It's the punishment that Satoshi left me.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:18.480
Wow!

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:19.700
The issue is this one.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:24.550
Why the gloves?

00:14:24.550 --> 00:14:27.290
I don't want to get her
treasured memento dirty.

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:28.510
Memento...

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.890
Maybe it sounds different somewhere?

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.090
Or maybe there's a
subtle colour difference?

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:43.290
The smell, maybe?

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:46.670
It smells weird!

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:49.080
Let me see that.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:52.830
You're right.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.630
Yeah, I think it's oil paint.

00:14:58.380 --> 00:14:59.500
I knew it.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:04.110
So he painted a regular Rubik's cube
white using oil paint?

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:04.880
Yeah.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:09.100
It looked like one of Narushima-san's
parents is an oil painter.

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:10.720
How do you know?

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:15.610
You got distracted by food and let something
important slip by unnoticed, like always.

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:17.540
Hey, I noticed the smell!

00:15:17.540 --> 00:15:20.150
Is there anything else you've noticed?

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.690
Even the slightest of details could help.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:29.270
This doesn't have Satoshi-kun's
signature on it anywhere.

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:34.700
Give it up and go
apologise to Narushima.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:37.620
Tell her you couldn't solve it,
but you want her to join anyway.

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:40.970
We have till tomorrow.

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:45.940
But if we don't know what the solution is,
how are we supposed to solve it?

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:48.800
Still, there's bound to be a right answer,

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.760
as long as it's a puzzle.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:52.650
Kusakabe-sensei...

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:55.520
So this is the Rubik's cube
everyone's talking about.

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:56.360
Yes.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:16:01.690
The person who can tell us its answer
is no longer in this world.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:10.660
If its creator knew that they'd be gone
by the time it was solved,

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:17.130
they must've left some way
to prove when that had happened.

00:16:17.130 --> 00:16:19.160
On this cube?

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:25.880
This cube is probably made so that the proof
will show up by itself when it's solved.

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:31.300
I think the steps that need to be taken to
find that proof are the crux of this puzzle.

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:36.220
This is no punishment.

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:38.730
Con amore.

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:40.390
Connor Moray?

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:42.260
It's a musical term.

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:43.870
It means "with love".

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:47.610
This is a puzzle that was made with love.

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:53.490
Did you solve it?

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:55.110
We're going to solve it now.

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:56.460
And you'll solve it with us.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:00.120
You called this cube a punishment, didn't you?

00:17:02.340 --> 00:17:03.400
That's right.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.930
Satoshi couldn't make friends
because he was ill.

00:17:07.930 --> 00:17:12.340
Puzzles were his source of
self-esteem and emotional support.

00:17:12.340 --> 00:17:15.660
He would make puzzles,
and I would solve them.

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:17.140
That's how it was for years...

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.640
Until I joined the concert band in junior high.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.450
I was having so much fun
that I left him all alone.

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:32.060
So this unsolvable puzzle
is a message from him to me.

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:36.160
Saying he wants to cause me trouble,
and that he won't forgive me.

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:39.490
You're right.
This cube is a message from Satoshi-kun.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:41.910
Let's begin.

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:50.050
Right now, this cube is in a scrambled state,
and all six sides are white.

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:51.340
This is the start line.

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:57.850
But no matter how much you turn it,
you'll always go back to square one.

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:02.060
Do you know why Satoshi-kun
didn't tell you what the solution was?

00:18:02.990 --> 00:18:09.820
Because he wanted you to take a
step forward from this scrambled state.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:11.650
But that's impossible.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:13.440
Chika-chan, Nishikawa-san.

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:16.540
Wh-What are you doing?

00:18:16.540 --> 00:18:17.820
Forgive me, Miyo-chan.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:20.160
I'll be done in three minutes.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:21.950
Trust me.

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:24.160
What do you think you're doing?

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:30.710
Stop it!

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:32.960
Hey, do you hear me?!

00:18:33.340 --> 00:18:35.450
Please, no!

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.930
Let go! Let me go!

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:50.750
All done.

00:18:52.570 --> 00:18:54.380
Why would you do this?

00:18:54.380 --> 00:18:57.320
It's time for you to move on, Narushima-san.

00:18:58.130 --> 00:18:59.280
Shut up.

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:03.620
Are you going to use your sorrow
as a shield for the rest of your life?

00:19:04.230 --> 00:19:08.610
It's only been a year since
Satoshi died, you know.

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:10.380
It's already been one whole year.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:15.130
A year in our lives is more significant
than a year in the life of an adult.

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:20.800
Haruta!

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:21.890
Stay away!

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:28.160
This cube is a message
that Satoshi-kun left for you!

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:31.730
No matter what happens,
never stop in your tracks.

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:34.090
Keep moving forward.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:37.240
He wants you to paint this
pure-white world in your own colours.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.450
And this is the first step.

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:56.650
Take a good look.

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:02.470
Your little brother sends his best wishes.

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:07.830
Satoshi...

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:05.770
Any signature on that cube
could only have been under the white.

00:21:05.770 --> 00:21:08.580
So I thought about how to strip it off.

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:13.270
The squares that were written on
had linen applied on them

00:21:13.270 --> 00:21:15.560
and were coated with zinc white.

00:21:16.510 --> 00:21:19.730
The other squares also had linen applied

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:22.120
but were coated in a different white paint.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:26.390
When you paint over zinc white
using an oil-based paint,

00:21:26.390 --> 00:21:27.260
it will peel off.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:31.770
But the paint needs to dry
before that happens.

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:33.190
It usually takes an entire day.

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:38.130
Yes. I'd already painted over it
in white yesterday,

00:21:38.130 --> 00:21:41.110
so that it would conveniently
peel off in front of Narushima-san.

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:42.740
Impressive.

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:45.630
I couldn't have done it
without Homura-san's hints.

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:47.830
<i>There's that goody-two-shoes act again.</i>

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:51.120
What will Narushima-san do now?

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:53.800
That's for her to decide.

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:01.260
A promise is a promise.

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:08.420
Oh, come on.

00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:34.390
Where am I supposed to take
my first step towards?

00:23:34.390 --> 00:23:36.330
Why is Maren in the drama club?

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:37.290
Challenge accepted.

00:23:37.290 --> 00:23:38.850
Yes, I call it...

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:40.400
"Exit Game"?
